          ":question/id": "b444fd71-00fa-57c3-8df1-8666ae2725d8",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-44380",
          ":question/problem": "A line $l$ is parallel to $6x-2y=11.$ A possible equation for $l$ is",
          ":question/worked-solution": "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $6x-2y=11,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-2y & =11 \\\\ \\frac{6}{2}x-\\frac{2}{2}y & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ 3x-y & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ -y & =-3x+\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ y & =3x-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $3.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $3$ is $y=3x-8.$"
        }
